---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/article.css';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="About — fake frontend"
	description="A warm, careful blog about the backend craft — TypeScript, Docker and Node.js."
	active="about"
>
	<article class="article">
		<div class="wrap art-head">
			<div class="crumbs"><span>About</span></div>
			<h1>The notebook of a backend engineer.</h1>
			<p class="standfirst">
				A warm, careful place to write down the things that actually ship.
			</p>
		</div>
		<div class="wrap art-body">
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					<strong>fake frontend</strong> is a personal blog about the backend
					craft — TypeScript, Docker and Node.js, and whatever else is worth
					writing down. The premise is simple: most guides tell you what to type;
					these let you <em>run</em> it. Every code snippet on the site is editable
					and executes for real, right in your browser, via WebContainers — so
					there’s no “it works on my machine.”
				</p>
				<p>
					New guides land most weeks. No newsletter funnels, no “10x your career” —
					just slow, careful deep dives into the parts of the stack that reward
					attention. If that’s your thing, <a href="/#about">subscribe</a> or grab
					the <a href="/rss.xml">RSS feed</a>.
				</p>
			</div>
			<aside class="toc-rail"></aside>
		</div>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>
